SWEET Mali job queue, a ring of N parked jobs (patch 0227, T-e395 one slot, T-b1c0 ring, G-F55; boot-arg sweetmalipend=N, N=0..4, DEFAULT OFF = same behaviour as before, 1 = the one-slot queue measured in T-e395). With the knob on, sweet_mali_submit no longer blocks behind the running job while fewer than N jobs are parked: it parks the job in a FIFO ring (seqno assigned) and returns; finish() starts the oldest parked job right after the running job completes through the same helper sm_start_locked() that the direct path now uses (moved verbatim: same clean-slot check, same JS_HEAD_NEXT/AFFINITY_NEXT/CONFIG_NEXT/COMMAND_NEXT writes, same order; no new register, value or clock). Still one job on the hardware at a time. A parked job behind a failed/faulted job, a dead device or a refused clean-slot check is DROPPED together with every parked job behind it (each reported failed, EIO to its waiter, one `SWEET MALI GAP: queued job seq=N dropped` line each). Targets the serial swap loop of docs/reviews/T-e395-proof.md: lag 4.6 + render 11.7 ms with wait_us 5.1 ms still blocked behind the previous job. Log lines: `SWEET MALI: queued submit ON (sweetmalipend) depth=N`, per second `SWEET PERF: pend queued N started N dropped N depth N maxparked N` (with sweetperf=1). BO close/unmap wait for the client last seqno, which counts queued jobs (SweetMali.cpp fLastSeq). Code: bsd/dev/sweet_mali.c. Needs patches 0145, 0186, 0212, 0222.
